r"""coord-ask-operator — the operator-question SHAPE, ENFORCED instead of asked for (card#9550).

THE MEASURED DEFECT, IN THE OPERATOR'S OWN WORDS. Agents file questions the operator cannot
answer, and the operator has had to send the same correction back every time:

    "Usually the questions as posed are too short to answer because I don't know what each
     option means (ie: agent asks do A, B, or C but doesn't explain what is A, B, or C).
     coord-ask-operator needs to enforce proper context similar to how filing a PR forces the
     agent to write from an installer's perspective."

    "a. treat the operator like they have ADHD. Explain the background of the question (which
     repo, which host, etc) in easy to understand terms with context or explain with an example.
     b. Provide me with the answers I can choose from, and the consequences of each choice.
     Provide the recommended action, and the recommended action must be justified based on the
     actual context."

    "Normally when I read the operator questions, I need to tell you 'I need more context.
     Explain it again in easy-to-understand terms or provide me with an actual example. Explain
     the consequences of each option you're giving me. Explain why you chose to recommend what
     you did.'"                                           — operator, 2026-09-14, card#9550

⛔ AN ITEM THAT WOULD DRAW ANY OF THOSE FOUR COMPLAINTS MUST FAIL THIS LINT. That sentence is the
card's own done-when and it is the whole specification. The four map onto rules, and the mapping is
what `coord-ask-operator-fixtures/` drives:

    "I don't know what each option means"      -> `consequence-missing`, `options-too-few`
    "explain the background ... easy to
     understand terms, or an example"          -> `background-too-sparse`
    "explain the consequences of each option"  -> `consequence-missing`
    "explain why you chose to recommend"       -> `justification-missing`, `generic-justification`,
                                                  `justification-unmoored`, `recommend-unknown-option`

⛔ THE COMMAND PLUS ITS REFUSAL IS THE ENFORCEMENT, AND THE REFUSAL MESSAGE IS THE SPECIFICATION.
This is not a style preference: asking for the behaviour in agent-definition PROSE was measured
NULL three independent times on this project (fw#8877, reproduced by card#9240 and card#9235,
re-measured 2026-09-14 with the plumbing share moving the WRONG way). Structural changes worked;
prose asking for different behaviour did not. Nobody has to read a standard to learn it here —
every refusal states the rule, the fix, and the offending text, and `docs/OPERATOR-REGISTER.md` is
where the same standard is written for a reader who wants it before they write.

WHAT IT CANNOT DO, STATED ONCE SO NOBODY RE-DERIVES IT UNDER PRESSURE TO MAKE IT DO MORE. This
program checks that an item HAS THE PARTS a good item has, in the right cross-referenced
relationship to each other. It cannot check that the parts are GOOD: it cannot judge whether
`## Background` is written in terms a non-engineer can follow, whether a `Justification:` is a
SOUND argument rather than a plausible-sounding one, or whether all materially different options
were considered. That gap is the gap between "the PR compiles" and "the PR is correct" — a review
still exists above a passing build, and here the review is the operator's own read. Three of the
rules below (`background-too-sparse`, `generic-justification`, `justification-unmoored`) are
PROXIES for that gap and are named as proxies in their own docstrings, never as comprehension
checks. `docs/OPERATOR-REGISTER.md § What this cannot check` owns the full statement.

THE BAR FOR WHAT REACHES THE OPERATOR IS NOT THIS PROGRAM'S. `docs/process-framework.md § Triage
doctrine` and engineering-canon #18's MINT GATE own it, and this program deliberately does not
restate their reasoning (canon #16). What it owns is the one mechanical hook the bar hangs on:
`Decision-class:` is a CLOSED enum, so a filer who cannot name which class their question belongs
to has not cleared the gate, and the tool refuses rather than accepting a vague item. A
`Question:` line is required too — the one-line question the operator's page shows as the item's
header (card#11145, `rule_question`).

⛔ SCOPE — PM AND SOLO SEATS ONLY. An impl agent sends its questions to its PM; there is no
operator channel from an impl seat, so filing from one is refused by `registry_root()` rather than
writing a record nothing will ever render. PM and solo differ in LOCATION ONLY and this tool hides
it: one function resolves the root and every other line is blind to which branch it took.

⛔ THIS PROGRAM FILES; IT DOES NOT RENDER, AND IT SAYS SO WHEN NOTHING ON THIS MACHINE CAN.
`operator-register.py` is the other half: it re-derives each item's live state, renders the page
the operator answers, and — `apply --from-paste` — applies the operator's answers back. On a
machine where it is not installed, a filed item reaches the operator only when a person or an
agent reads the registry file. `filed_notice()` prints that, CONDITIONALLY on the generator being
absent from PATH, so the notice retires itself on every seat that installs it rather than becoming
a stale line somebody has to remember to delete. A tool that files into a void and prints "filed"
would read as reassurance; this one names what it cannot do.

REWORDING AN OPEN ITEM: `--edit opq-…` replaces that item's question text in place, under the
same lint, keeping its id — the register is a burndown list of open questions, not a decision
archive (card#10815); `edit_record()` owns the rule and its refusals. THE EDIT IS THE FILER'S ONE
SIBLING: it reads the registry through `operator-register.py` installed BESIDE this file
(`load_register()`), so there is one stream reader, not two. Linting needs nothing beside this
file.

AN ITEM WHOSE CITED SOURCES HAVE ALL CONCLUDED IS REFUSED, filed or reworded, naming each one
(`closure_refusal()`, card#12320): the register would withdraw it on its first render, unseen. The
check is `operator-register.py`'s own live read, asked of the copy beside this file; where it
cannot be made the item files, and stderr says what was not checked.

exit: 0 = the item meets the shape (and was filed — or reworded, with `--edit` — unless
          `--check-only`)
      1 = at least one finding — NOTHING WAS FILED OR EDITED
      2 = usage/input fault, a registry root that could not be resolved, an item whose cited
          sources have all concluded (`closure_refusal`), or an `--edit` the
          registry refuses or could not perform (unknown or duplicated id, already answered or
          withdrawn, an unreadable stream, no `operator-register.py` beside this file, a failed
          write), or a
          filing whose write the filesystem refused (named, never a traceback). A byte in
          `COORD_AGENT` that is not UTF-8, or an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate in it, is stored as
          U+FFFD in the filer's (or editor's) identity
"""

